DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/german-studies-lecture-series
 -hannes-bajohr-worlds-in-ai-and-the-novel\n\nPlease join German Studies Le
 cture Series for Worlds in AI and the Novel\, a conversation with Hannes B
 ajohr (UC Berkeley).\n\n“A world—nothing less—is the theme and postu
 late of the novel\,” Hans Blumenberg wrote in 1963\, as AI research was 
 beginning to develop its own “world models.” Today\, these literary an
 d computational senses of world converge in LLMs\, whose latent spaces can
  generate narratives with striking coherence but often weak causality. Dra
 wing on literary traditions from realism to modernism and on his own exper
 iments with a fine-tuned German-language model\, Professor Bajohr asks how
  novels and LLMs construct worlds through networks of relations\, inferenc
 es\, and expectations. The talk uses this comparison to situate AI-generat
 ed fiction between claims of machine understanding and dismissals of mere 
 mimicry.\n\nRSVP for the Hannes Bajohr event here.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
